{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"If an ETF holds derivatives that an authorized participant (AP) cannot practicably transfer under the fund’s dealing arrangements, the fund may accept cash instead (or use a mixed basket). The fund can then enter, close, or adjust those positions itself. By contrast, readily transferable securities can often be delivered in kind, letting the AP assemble or receive the basket and helping the fund avoid trading costs and potential tax consequences.\n\nThis is a practical explanation, not a rule that all derivatives require cash or all securities are delivered in kind. The prospectus and basket policies govern; funds may use cash for other reasons, and derivatives may sometimes be transferable. Cash transactions can also impose trading costs, market impact, and tax effects on the fund and its shareholders."}}
